@page "/"
@using PullbackStrategyLab.Web.Shell
@model IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "What this is";
    ExperimentView e = Model.Experiment;
}
@*
    The front page, from 7.20. Written for somebody who has never seen the lab before: what the
    experiment is, how far it has got, and what would settle it.

    Every figure here is per direction. There is no total on this page, and no arithmetic in this
    file produces one.
    see: Long and short are never pooled into one figure
    see: A surface a person opens is a screen, and the navigation names the way in apart from the research screens
*@
<h1>An experiment in whether one price pattern makes money</h1>

<p class="lede">Every evening this lab looks at about two thousand US stocks, writes down the ones it
   would buy or sell short tomorrow and why, then checks the next day whether it was right.
   <b>The money is imaginary and always will be.</b></p>

@if (e.IsAbsent)
{
    <div class="empty-state">
        <p>The record cannot be read: @e.Absent.</p>
    </div>
}
else
{
    <h2>Where it has got to</h2>

    <dl class="panels">
        <div class="panel">
            <dt>Patterns to buy</dt>
            <dd class="num long">@e.Long.PatternsRecorded.ToString("N0")</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
            <dt>Patterns to sell short</dt>
            <dd class="num short">@e.Short.PatternsRecorded.ToString("N0")</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
            <dt>Plans to buy</dt>
            <dd class="num long">@e.Long.PlansWritten.ToString("N0")</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
            <dt>Plans to sell short</dt>
            <dd class="num short">@e.Short.PlansWritten.ToString("N0")</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
            <dt>Trades taken, buying</dt>
            <dd class="num long">@e.Long.TradesClosed.ToString("N0")</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
            <dt>Trades taken, short</dt>
            <dd class="num short">@e.Short.TradesClosed.ToString("N0")</dd>
        </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="muted">Buying and short selling are counted apart here and everywhere else in this
       lab. They are never added into one number, because a short carries a borrowing cost and a
       different risk that a long does not.</p>

    @if (e.Long.TradesClosed == 0 && e.Short.TradesClosed == 0)
    {
        <div class="caveat">
            <b>No trades yet, and that is a result rather than a fault.</b> A plan only becomes a
            trade if the price does a particular thing the next day: dip to a level and recover.
            So far none has, on either side.
        </div>
    }

    <h2>How much of the record exists</h2>

    <p class="lede">The lab holds <b>@e.Evenings evening@(e.Evenings == 1 ? "" : "s")</b> of
       detections, between @e.FirstEvening and @e.LatestEvening.</p>

    @if (e.RecordHasAGap)
    {
        <div class="caveat">
            <b>There is a gap in it, and the gap cannot be filled.</b> Those dates span
            @e.WeekdaysInSpan weekdays and the lab holds @e.Evenings of them, so it was not running
            for the rest. A night nobody recorded is gone for good: the prices can be bought back
            afterwards, but what the lab would have flagged on the evening cannot.
        </div>
    }

    <h2>How the last evening went</h2>

    <p class="lede">The evening of @e.Long.Funnel.Evening. The two sides are drawn separately and
       never added together.</p>

    @foreach (ExperimentSideView side in new[] { e.Long, e.Short })
    {
        <section class="side">
            <h3>Stocks @side.InWords</h3>
            <ol class="rungs">
                <li class="rung">
                    <span class="count">@side.Funnel.Examined.ToString("N0")</span>
                    <span class="what">looked at<em>everything liquid enough to trade</em></span>
                    <span class="bar"><i style="width:@side.Funnel.WidthOf(side.Funnel.Examined)"></i></span>
                </li>
                <li class="rung">
                    <span class="count">@side.Funnel.Recorded.ToString("N0")</span>
                    <span class="what">had moved, then paused<em>the shape the lab is hunting for</em></span>
                    <span class="bar"><i style="width:@side.Funnel.WidthOf(side.Funnel.Recorded)"></i></span>
                </li>
                <li class="rung">
                    <span class="count">@side.Funnel.Passed.ToString("N0")</span>
                    <span class="what">passed every check<em>the rest are kept, each with the reason it failed</em></span>
                    <span class="bar"><i style="width:@side.Funnel.WidthOf(side.Funnel.Passed)"></i></span>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>
    }

    <p class="muted">The names that failed are kept rather than thrown away. That is what makes it
       possible to ask later whether a rule was refusing the right stocks, and it is why the
       <a href="/setups">Setups</a> screen lists far more names than the lab would ever buy.</p>

    @if (e.Risk is ExperimentRiskView risk)
    {
        <h2>What it is allowed to lose</h2>

        <p class="lede">One plan risks at most <b>@risk.Budget.ToString("C0")</b> of the lab's
           @risk.Equity.ToString("C0") of imaginary money, which is @risk.FractionInWords of it.
           That cap is applied to every plan before it is allowed to become an order.</p>
    }

    <h2>What would prove this wrong</h2>

    <p class="lede">If the stocks the lab picks do no better than stocks picked at random with a
       similar shape, the pattern is not real and the experiment has answered its question. That
       comparison runs from the first trade onwards and is the only thing that settles it. It is on
       the <a href="/scoreboard">Scoreboard</a>, and it is the number this lab exists to produce.</p>

    <p class="muted">The rules change as evidence arrives. The evening of @e.LatestEvening was
       scored under generation @e.LatestEveningGeneration, and every stored row records the
       generation that scored it, so an old evening can always be read under the rule that actually
       produced it.</p>
}
